# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: autocad-com-launch-bounded-by-dcom-timeout -- a slow AutoCAD start fails at DCOM's 120 s, whatever --timeout says
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-autocad-com-launch-beyond-dcom-timeout
#+DATE: 2026-11-05

* Symptom

On PF5S26BT (AutoCAD 2022, 2026-11-05), every --autocad --mode automation
and --autocad --epure run failed before READY:

: ERROR COM bridge: could not launch AutoCAD.Application.24.1: Un composant ActiveX ne peut pas creer un objet
: COM.STAGE=createobject
: COM.ERROR.DECIMAL=429

alfe exits 69 about 123 s after the launch, with --timeout 180 or 360. The
System log has one DCOM event 10010 per run: "Le serveur
{AA46BA8A-9825-40FD-8493-0BA3C4D5CEB5} ne s'est pas enregistre sur DCOM avant
la fin du temps imparti". The same machine reached READY in 17-42 s on
2026-11-03 (verify:epure-lifecycle:windows, jobs 17693490391, 17694882952);
on 2026-11-05 a run that succeeded needed 53 s and 108 s.

Seen in: verify:epure-lifecycle:windows (MR !512 / !513 and the pre-rebase
!476 commit e85db5ab, so not a code change), probe:cad-session-state:windows
(automation and epure rows FAIL, batch OK in 8 s).

* Cause

The bridge creates AutoCAD with CreateObject(progId). DCOM then starts the
LocalServer32 (acad.exe /Automation) and waits for it to register its class
object -- for 120 s, fixed by Windows, not configurable from the client.
AutoCAD registers only at the END of its startup. When that takes longer than
120 s, CreateObject returns 429 and the AutoCAD it started is abandoned.

Measured on the stalled process (in-session watcher, 2026-11-05): acad.exe
was up, responding, with its window "Autodesk AutoCAD 2022 - [Dessin1.dwg]"
at 45 s, and CPU-bound (66-79 s of CPU by 90 s) -- a slow start, not a hang,
no dialog. Not the cause: the licence server (reachable; the stall precedes
the licensing SDK registration), a disconnected session (console, active),
TRUSTEDPATHS growth (3 entries), IFEO / page heap, NGEN (every AutoCAD .NET
assembly loads as .ni.dll). Why AutoCAD's own start became 3-5x slower on
that machine is NOT established; it needs an in-session profile.

So alfe's --timeout, which is the user's statement of how long a start may
take, was silently capped at 120 s by a component alfe does not control.

* Fix

When no acad.exe is running, the bridge starts the server itself -- the
LocalServer32 command of the ProgID's CLSID, the same command DCOM would run
-- and polls GetObject(, progId) once a second until AutoCAD registers, for
the ready timeout alfe was given. It stops early, with its own diagnosis, if
the launched process exits. With another AutoCAD already running, GetObject
could return THAT instance, so the bridge keeps CreateObject there (alfe
launches a new instance by default: alfe-cad-launch-new-by-default).

* Acceptance

- The template launches the LocalServer32 command and polls GetObject for
  ${LAUNCH_SECS} (the ready timeout); a regression test fails on the old
  bridge.
- probe:cad-session-state:windows on PF5S26BT: the autocad automation and
  epure rows reach READY again.
